1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:02,008
Your script works.

2
00:00:02,138 --> 00:00:06,084
Then somebody clones the repository and gets an import
error.

3
00:00:06,214 --> 00:00:09,210
You send them three extra commands in a message.

4
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:13,240
A week later, somebody else needs the same three
commands.

5
00:00:13,370 --> 00:00:18,478
That is a setup problem you can fix before the project
gets complicated.

6
00:00:19,128 --> 00:00:21,728
Start with a terminal inside your project.

7
00:00:21,858 --> 00:00:28,071
Run pwd to check where you are, then git status to see
what Git knows about this directory.

8
00:00:28,201 --> 00:00:31,046
Relative paths start from your current directory.

9
00:00:31,176 --> 00:00:35,994
If a command cannot find a file, check that location
before changing the code.

10
00:00:36,644 --> 00:00:43,445
Create a virtual environment with python -m venv .venv.

11
00:00:43,575 --> 00:00:50,607
On macOS or Linux, activate it with source
.venv/bin/activate.

12
00:00:50,737 --> 00:00:57,141
On Windows PowerShell, use the activation script under
.venv/Scripts.

13
00:00:57,271 --> 00:01:01,485
Now python -m pip points at the installer for that
interpreter.

14
00:01:01,615 --> 00:01:07,072
Install your one dependency, then capture the versions in
requirements.txt.

15
00:01:07,202 --> 00:01:10,813
This gives the next person something specific to install.

16
00:01:11,463 --> 00:01:17,657
Before staging anything, add the environment directory,
local secrets, and raw data to

17
00:01:17,657 --> 00:01:18,450
.gitignore.

18
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:22,446
An ignore rule keeps an untracked file out of future
staging.

19
00:01:22,576 --> 00:01:25,281
It does not remove something you already committed.

20
00:01:25,411 --> 00:01:30,236
Think of it as the guest list at the door; it cannot
erase yesterday's attendance record.

21
00:01:30,886 --> 00:01:32,987
Now watch the three Git states.

22
00:01:33,117 --> 00:01:35,756
Edit a file: that changes your working tree.

23
00:01:35,886 --> 00:01:39,772
Run git add: that selects its current contents for your
next commit.

24
00:01:39,902 --> 00:01:43,553
Run git commit: that records the staged snapshot locally.

25
00:01:43,683 --> 00:01:46,040
A push transfers commits to a remote.

26
00:01:46,170 --> 00:01:51,956
Check git diff and git diff --staged so you know exactly
which snapshot you are recording.

27
00:01:52,606 --> 00:01:55,090
For the lab, keep your baseline small.

28
00:01:55,220 --> 00:02:00,403
Make a feature branch, add the environment check, README,
and decision note in separate

29
00:02:00,403 --> 00:02:04,156
meaningful commits, and review the pull request before
merging.

30
00:02:04,286 --> 00:02:09,292
Follow the course's three-commit exercise without
stretching one change into artificial

31
00:02:09,292 --> 00:02:09,752
commits.

32
00:02:10,402 --> 00:02:16,301
Then do the part that proves the README works: use a
fresh clone, create and activate a new

33
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:20,645
environment, install from the requirements file, and run
the check.

34
00:02:20,775 --> 00:02:24,722
Do not rely on the environment you already repaired by
hand.

35
00:02:25,372 --> 00:02:29,551
Your evidence is a successful clean setup and a
reviewable history.

36
00:02:29,681 --> 00:02:34,952
Before you move on, explain which files belong in Git,
which stay local, and where somebody

37
00:02:34,952 --> 00:02:38,137
would find the exact commands to reproduce your result.
